<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Phrygia</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 675 BCE (approx.) · capital Gordion</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Phrygia, the Iron Age kingdom of Gordion in central Anatolia, home of King Midas and the Gordian knot.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygians">Phrygians</a>, probably migrants from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Late Bronze Age collapse</a>, settled the plateau west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizilirmak">Kızılırmak</a>. By the 9th century BCE they had built a fortified capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordion</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakarya-river">Sakarya</a> river, with a citadel of megaron halls. Assyrian texts call the people Mushki.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Phrygian kings ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordion</a> and built huge burial mounds; Tumulus MM held a king with bronze vessels and fine wooden furniture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">Mita</a> of Assyrian records, was a rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> and sent gifts to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delphi">Delphi</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygians">Phrygians</a> wrote in an alphabet adapted from Greek and worshipped the mother goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cybele">Matar</a>, later Cybele.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>According to Greek tradition, Cimmerian invaders overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> in the early 7th century BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a> killed himself; the great destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordion</a> itself is now dated c. 800 BCE. Phrygia then fell under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, though its language and cults survived for centuries.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-phrygia.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Phrygia" width="320" height="294"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Amizzoni~commonswiki assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkey_ancient_region_map_phrygia.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 675 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gordion</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Midas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Phrygian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Phrygian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Phrygia, Mushki</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-788&amp;focus=kingdom-of-phrygia" aria-label="Kingdom of Phrygia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5448 779 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Phrygia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5768 1035L5697 1052"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5697 1052h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5768 1035h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5768 1035h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-788&amp;focus=kingdom-of-phrygia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

